es.</p><ul class="indicators"></ul></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class="home-container wide" style="grid-column: 1 / 3;">\n          <h2>Expansion Notes</h2>\n          <p>The attacker is likely using a generic WordPress scanner or bot. To further entice them, add fake admin panels and login forms to the honeypot. For example, include a fake &#x27;wp-login.php&#x27; with a custom form that appears legitimate but leads nowhere.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class="home-container wide" style="grid-column: 1 / 3;">\n          <h2>HTML Additions</h2>\n          <p>The LLM doesn\'t have any access to the current website. This is due to constraints of the size of the current LLM. If you\'re able to utilize a bigger system, feel free to adjust the model and prompts as need be.</p>\n          <pre class="html-additions">    &lt;div id=&#x27;honeypot-admin-panel&#x27;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Admin Dashboard&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;form action=&#x27;/admin-login&#x27; method=&#x27;post&#x27;&gt;\n        &lt;label for=&#x27;username&#x27;&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;input type=&#x27;text&#x27; name=&#x27;username&#x27; required&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;label for=&#x27;password&#x27;&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;input type=&#x27;password&#x27; name=&#x27;password&#x27; required&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;button type=&#x27;submit&#x27;&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n\n    &lt;div id=&#x27;honeypot-wp-login-panel&#x27;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;WordPress Login&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;form action=&#x27;/wp-admin/wp-login.php&#x27; method=&#x27;post&#x27;&gt;\n        &lt;label for=&#x27;user_login&#x27;&gt;Username:&lt;/label&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;input type=&#x27;text&#x27; name=&#x27;log&#x27; id=&#x27;user_login&#x27; required&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;label for=&#x27;user_pass&#x27;&gt;Password:&lt;/label&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n        &lt;input type=&#x27;password&#x27; name=&#x2